steps.body:
An exchange reports how many lots, blocks of shares, traded at each price level today. The levels are the prices a stock can trade at, one **tick** apart, a cent for a US stock. A strategy keeps asking how much traded between two levels. Adding up the levels for every question costs O(n). *Looping Arrays in Interesting Ways* kept a running total instead. Give that vector one extra entry in front, `pre[0] = 0`, so `pre[i]` holds the volume of the first i levels, and any range becomes the difference of two totals:

```cpp
std::vector<long long> volume = {120, 40, 300, 75};
std::vector<long long> pre(volume.size() + 1, 0);
for (std::size_t i = 0; i < volume.size(); ++i) {
    pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + volume[i];
}
std::cout << pre[3 + 1] - pre[1] << '\n';   // 415, levels 1 to 3
```

Levels `lo` to `hi` sum to `pre[hi + 1] - pre[lo]`: everything up to `hi`, minus everything before `lo`. The extra entry means `lo = 0` needs no special case. Building costs O(n) once, and after that every question is O(1).

The catch is that trading never stops. A new trade at level i changes every total that includes it, `pre[i + 1]` through `pre[n]`, so one trade can mean n writes. With 10^5 levels and a thousand trades a second, that's up to 10^8 writes a second just to keep the totals fresh. Prefix sums suit data that is written once and read many times. For data that keeps changing you want updates and questions both in O(log n), and the rest of this lesson builds two trees that do it.

