#include <algorithm>
#include <climits>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct Span {
    int low;
    int high;
};

Span merge(Span a, Span b) {
    return {std::min(a.low, b.low), std::max(a.high, b.high)};
}

struct RangeTree {
    int n;
    std::vector<Span> node;

    explicit RangeTree(const std::vector<int>& prices) : n(static_cast<int>(prices.size())), node(4 * n) {
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            set(1, 0, n - 1, i, prices[i]);
        }
    }

    void set(int k, int lo, int hi, int i, int price) {
        if (lo == hi) {
            node[k] = {price, price};
            return;
        }
        const int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (i <= mid) {
            set(2 * k, lo, mid, i, price);
        } else {
            set(2 * k + 1, mid + 1, hi, i, price);
        }
        node[k] = merge(node[2 * k], node[2 * k + 1]);
    }

    Span query(int k, int lo, int hi, int l, int r) const {
        if (r < lo || hi < l) {
            return {INT_MAX, INT_MIN};
        }
        if (l <= lo && hi <= r) {
            return node[k];
        }
        const int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        return merge(query(2 * k, lo, mid, l, r), query(2 * k + 1, mid + 1, hi, l, r));
    }
};

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::vector<int> prices(n);
    for (int& p : prices) {
        std::cin >> p;
    }
    RangeTree tree(prices);
    std::string op;
    int a = 0;
    int b = 0;
    while (std::cin >> op >> a >> b) {
        if (op == "C") {
            tree.set(1, 0, n - 1, a, b);
        } else {
            const Span s = tree.query(1, 0, n - 1, a, b);
            std::cout << a << " to " << b << ": low " << s.low << ", high " << s.high
                      << ", range " << s.high - s.low << '\n';
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
Some updates cover a whole range: a payment lowers the cash balance of every day from its date on, or a job holds cores for a block of hours. As point updates that's O(n log n) per range. **Lazy propagation** makes a range update as cheap as a query by putting work off until something needs it.

Each node keeps a `pending` add, often called its lazy tag, next to its minimum. When an update covers a node's whole range, apply it to that node and stop there:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

std::vector<long long> low(4, 100);
std::vector<long long> pending(4, 0);

{{code}}

int main() {
    apply(1, -30);
    std::cout << "node 1: low " << low[1] << ", pending " << pending[1] << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
void apply(int k, long long v) {
    low[k] += v;       // every value below k moved by v, so their minimum did too
    pending[k] += v;   // and k's children still owe it
}
```

The children are stale now. Before a later update or query goes below node k, `push(k)` applies the pending add to both children and clears it. Updates and queries only step into the children of a node they partly overlap, and that's exactly where they push, so nothing ever reads a stale value. An update visits the same O(log n) nodes as a query and recomputes each partly covered node from its children on the way back up.

A node that is partly overlapped always has two children, since a single position is either in a range or out of it, so a push never runs off the bottom of the tree. A range add with range sums works the same way, except that `apply` adds `v` times the number of positions to the sum. Booking systems, schedulers, and risk checks that ask whether every slot from l to r has room can run on this tree.

